/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 161 0 R
/T (The player controls a small character through three worlds named Forest World, Desert World and Ice Kingdom. In each world the player moves left and right, jumps onto platforms, collects coins to increase the score, and either avoids or jumps on moving enemies. The player starts with three lives, can reach a checkpoint in each world, and can use three temporary power-ups called Speed, Shield and Super Jump. The third world ends with a final boss who has three health points and must be defeated before the game can be completed. The game also contains a main menu, an instructions screen, a level-completion screen, a game-over screen, a victory screen and a restart option.)
/E (The player controls a small character through three worlds named Forest World, Desert World and Ice Kingdom. In each world the player moves left and right, jumps onto platforms, collects coins to increase the score, and either avoids or jumps on moving enemies. The player starts with three lives, can reach a checkpoint in each world, and can use three temporary power-ups called Speed, Shield and Super Jump. The third world ends with a final boss who has three health points and must be defeated before the game can be completed. The game also contains a main menu, an instructions screen, a level-completion screen, a game-over screen, a victory screen and a restart option.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 161 0 R
/T (The complete program is written in a single source file, main.cpp. It uses structures, an enumeration, vectors, functions, global variables, conditions and loops, together with a timer-driven game loop, keyboard input and an off-screen drawing buffer for smooth graphics. The game is compiled with MinGW g++ and was developed in Visual Studio Code for Windows 10 / Windows 11. The program follows a structured, function-based style and does not define classes, so object-oriented features such as inheritance and polymorphism are not part of this version. The outcome of the project is a complete and playable game that helped the author practise core C++ programming, game logic, collision detection and Windows graphics programming.)
/E (The complete program is written in a single source file, main.cpp. It uses structures, an enumeration, vectors, functions, global variables, conditions and loops, together with a timer-driven game loop, keyboard input and an off-screen drawing buffer for smooth graphics. The game is compiled with MinGW g++ and was developed in Visual Studio Code for Windows 10 / Windows 11. The program follows a structured, function-based style and does not define classes, so object-oriented features such as inheritance and polymorphism are not part of this version. The outcome of the project is a complete and playable game that helped the author practise core C++ programming, game logic, collision detection and Windows graphics programming.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
