/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 166 0 R
/T (Player movement, gravity and jumping. The player's position is stored in playerX and playerY, and the vertical speed is stored in velocityY. The normal walking speed is 5 pixels per update. Pressing a jump key while the player is standing on a surface \(onGround is true\) gives an upward speed of -16. In every update gravity adds 1 to velocityY, and the falling speed is limited to 15. The world is 2300 pixels wide, and the player cannot walk outside it. The value cameraX follows the player so that the 900-pixel-wide window always shows the part of the world around the character. Platforms and collision detection. Platforms are stored in a vector of the structure Platform. The ground is a long platform at the bottom of the world, and each world adds five floating platforms. The function Collision checks whether two rectangles overlap by comparing their edges; this is the basic form of rectangle \(bounding box\) collision detection. When the falling player overlaps a platform, the player is placed on top of it and onGround becomes true. If the player is moving upwards and hits the underside of a platform, the player is pushed below it and the upward movement stops.)
/E (Player movement, gravity and jumping. The player's position is stored in playerX and playerY, and the vertical speed is stored in velocityY. The normal walking speed is 5 pixels per update. Pressing a jump key while the player is standing on a surface \(onGround is true\) gives an upward speed of -16. In every update gravity adds 1 to velocityY, and the falling speed is limited to 15. The world is 2300 pixels wide, and the player cannot walk outside it. The value cameraX follows the player so that the 900-pixel-wide window always shows the part of the world around the character. Platforms and collision detection. Platforms are stored in a vector of the structure Platform. The ground is a long platform at the bottom of the world, and each world adds five floating platforms. The function Collision checks whether two rectangles overlap by comparing their edges; this is the basic form of rectangle \(bounding box\) collision detection. When the falling player overlaps a platform, the player is placed on top of it and onGround becomes true. If the player is moving upwards and hits the underside of a platform, the player is pushed below it and the upward movement stops.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
336 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 166 0 R
/T (Coin collection and score. Every world contains 20 coins, which are placed by a loop in LoadStage in a repeating pattern of three heights. When the player's rectangle overlaps a coin, the coin is marked as collected and 10 points are added to the score. The other score rewards are 20 points for defeating an enemy, 50 points for reaching the checkpoint, 50 points for each successful hit on the boss and 200 extra points for defeating the boss. The score, lives and world number are shown at the top-left corner of the screen.)
/E (Coin collection and score. Every world contains 20 coins, which are placed by a loop in LoadStage in a repeating pattern of three heights. When the player's rectangle overlaps a coin, the coin is marked as collected and 10 points are added to the score. The other score rewards are 20 points for defeating an enemy, 50 points for reaching the checkpoint, 50 points for each successful hit on the boss and 200 extra points for defeating the boss. The score, lives and world number are shown at the top-left corner of the screen.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
