344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 167 0 R
/T (Lives and checkpoints. The player starts the game with three lives. LoseLife reduces the number of lives by one. If lives remain, the player is placed back at the starting position \(x = 80\) or, if the checkpoint has been reached, at the checkpoint position \(x = 1100\). When the player's x position passes 1050 for the first time in a world, the checkpoint is saved, 50 points are added and the message CHECKPOINT SAVED is displayed. The code also contains a test for falling below the screen, but in the present levels the ground is continuous, so lives are mainly lost by touching enemies or the boss. When the number of lives reaches zero, the game changes to the game-over screen.)
/E (Lives and checkpoints. The player starts the game with three lives. LoseLife reduces the number of lives by one. If lives remain, the player is placed back at the starting position \(x = 80\) or, if the checkpoint has been reached, at the checkpoint position \(x = 1100\). When the player's x position passes 1050 for the first time in a world, the checkpoint is saved, 50 points are added and the message CHECKPOINT SAVED is displayed. The code also contains a test for falling below the screen, but in the present levels the ground is continuous, so lives are mainly lost by touching enemies or the boss. When the number of lives reaches zero, the game changes to the game-over screen.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 167 0 R
/T (Power-ups. The game has three temporary power-ups. In the supplied code they are activated by pressing S \(Speed\), H \(Shield\) or J \(Super Jump\) during play. The active power-up is stored in powerType and lasts for powerTimer = 300 updates, which is about five seconds. Speed raises the walking speed from 5 to 9 pixels per update. Shield makes LoseLife return without removing a life and draws a light-blue circle around the player. Super Jump raises the jump speed from -16 to -22. Only one power-up is active at a time, and its name is shown on the screen. In this version the power-ups are triggered by key presses; there are no power-up items placed in the levels.)
/E (Power-ups. The game has three temporary power-ups. In the supplied code they are activated by pressing S \(Speed\), H \(Shield\) or J \(Super Jump\) during play. The active power-up is stored in powerType and lasts for powerTimer = 300 updates, which is about five seconds. Speed raises the walking speed from 5 to 9 pixels per update. Shield makes LoseLife return without removing a life and draws a light-blue circle around the player. Super Jump raises the jump speed from -16 to -22. Only one power-up is active at a time, and its name is shown on the screen. In this version the power-ups are triggered by key presses; there are no power-up items placed in the levels.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
346 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 167 0 R
/T (Boss health and the final battle. In World 3 a purple boss is placed near the end of the level. It moves slowly between x = 1800 and x = 1980 and has a health value bossHealth of 3, which is shown above it as BOSS HP. Jumping on the boss from above reduces its health by one, makes the player bounce, adds 50 points and starts a short cooldown of 35 updates so that one landing is not counted several times. When the health reaches zero the boss disappears and 200 bonus points are added. Touching the boss in any other way calls LoseLife. While the boss is alive, the message DEFEAT THE BOSS FIRST is displayed and World 3 cannot be completed.)
/E (Boss health and the final battle. In World 3 a purple boss is placed near the end of the level. It moves slowly between x = 1800 and x = 1980 and has a health value bossHealth of 3, which is shown above it as BOSS HP. Jumping on the boss from above reduces its health by one, makes the player bounce, adds 50 points and starts a short cooldown of 35 updates so that one landing is not counted several times. When the health reaches zero the boss disappears and 200 bonus points are added. Touching the boss in any other way calls LoseLife. While the boss is alive, the message DEFEAT THE BOSS FIRST is displayed and World 3 cannot be completed.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
347 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 167 0 R
/T (Level transitions, game over and victory. Each world ends with a finish flag at x = 2140, drawn as a white pole with a red flag. Reaching it in World 1 or World 2 calls CompleteStage; in World 3 it works only after the boss has been defeated. The game then shows a level-complete screen \(for example, WORLD 1 COMPLETED!\) for 100 updates, about 1.6 seconds, before NextStage loads the next world. After World 3 the victory screen appears with the final score. If the lives reach zero, the game-over screen appears instead. On both screens, R restarts the game and Esc returns to the main menu.)
/E (Level transitions, game over and victory. Each world ends with a finish flag at x = 2140, drawn as a white pole with a red flag. Reaching it in World 1 or World 2 calls CompleteStage; in World 3 it works only after the boss has been defeated. The game then shows a level-complete screen \(for example, WORLD 1 COMPLETED!\) for 100 updates, about 1.6 seconds, before NextStage loads the next world. After World 3 the victory screen appears with the final score. If the lives reach zero, the game-over screen appears instead. On both screens, R restarts the game and Esc returns to the main menu.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
348 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 167 0 R
/T (Graphics and appearance of the worlds. Everything on the screen is drawn with GDI. DrawRectangle fills a rectangle with a solid brush, DrawCircle draws an ellipse, DrawTextCenter creates an Arial font and writes centred text, and TextOutA writes the score line. The player is made from rectangles and a small circle, enemies are red circles with white eyes, coins are yellow circles and the finish flag is made from two rectangles. The Forest World has a light-blue sky and green trees, the Desert World has an orange sky and a yellow sun, and the Ice Kingdom has a pale-blue sky with pale circles that look like snow mounds. The platforms use the same brown body and green top in every world. Emoji characters are not used, because they did not display properly in the Windows graphics environment; shapes drawn with GDI are used instead.)
/E (Graphics and appearance of the worlds. Everything on the screen is drawn with GDI. DrawRectangle fills a rectangle with a solid brush, DrawCircle draws an ellipse, DrawTextCenter creates an Arial font and writes centred text, and TextOutA writes the score line. The player is made from rectangles and a small circle, enemies are red circles with white eyes, coins are yellow circles and the finish flag is made from two rectangles. The Forest World has a light-blue sky and green trees, the Desert World has an orange sky and a yellow sun, and the Ice Kingdom has a pale-blue sky with pale circles that look like snow mounds. The platforms use the same brown body and green top in every world. Emoji characters are not used, because they did not display properly in the Windows graphics environment; shapes drawn with GDI are used instead.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
