1408 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 193 0 R
/T (The author learned how a game loop works, how the position and speed of a character can be changed in every update, and how gravity and jumping can be created with simple arithmetic. Rectangle-based collision detection was used for platforms, coins, enemies and the boss, and it showed how the same idea can solve many different problems. Handling keyboard input with GetAsyncKeyState and window messages was another important part of the work, as was managing game states such as the menu, playing, level complete, game over and win screens.)
/E (The author learned how a game loop works, how the position and speed of a character can be changed in every update, and how gravity and jumping can be created with simple arithmetic. Rectangle-based collision detection was used for platforms, coins, enemies and the boss, and it showed how the same idea can solve many different problems. Handling keyboard input with GetAsyncKeyState and window messages was another important part of the work, as was managing game states such as the menu, playing, level complete, game over and win screens.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 193 0 R
/T (The project also introduced graphics programming with the Windows API and GDI. Drawing the whole game from rectangles, circles and text, and using an off-screen buffer for smooth display, showed how a window application works in Windows. Testing the game while it was running helped in finding and correcting mistakes in the game logic.)
/E (The project also introduced graphics programming with the Windows API and GDI. Drawing the whole game from rectangles, circles and text, and using an off-screen buffer for smooth display, showed how a window application works in Windows. Testing the game while it was running helped in finding and correcting mistakes in the game logic.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 193 0 R
/T (The program is a simple game and is written in a structured style with structures and functions. It does not use classes, inheritance or polymorphism, so it should not be seen as a full object-oriented design. The following ideas are possible future improvements and are not part of the present version: converting the structures into classes with member functions, adding collectible power-up items in the levels, adding sound effects, adding more worlds and enemy types, and saving the highest score.)
/E (The program is a simple game and is written in a structured style with structures and functions. It does not use classes, inheritance or polymorphism, so it should not be seen as a full object-oriented design. The following ideas are possible future improvements and are not part of the present version: converting the structures into classes with member functions, adding collectible power-up items in the levels, adding sound effects, adding more worlds and enemy types, and saving the highest score.)
