3001 0 obj
<<
/Title (Coin collection and score. Every world contains 20 coins, which are placed by a loop in LoadStage in a repeating pattern of three heights. When the player's rectangle overlaps a coin, the coin is marked as collected and 10 points are added to the score. The other score rewards are 20 points for defeating an enemy, 50 points for reaching the checkpoint, 50 points for each successful hit on the boss and 200 extra points for defeating the boss. The score, lives and world number are shown at the top-left corner of the screen.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2990 0 R
/Prev 3005 0 R
/Next 2997 0 R
>>
endobj
3002 0 obj
<<
/Title (Power-ups. The game has three temporary power-ups. In the supplied code they are activated by pressing S \(Speed\), H \(Shield\) or J \(Super Jump\) during play. The active power-up is stored in powerType and lasts for powerTimer = 300 updates, which is about five seconds. Speed raises the walking speed from 5 to 9 pixels per update. Shield makes LoseLife return without removing a life and draws a light-blue circle around the player. Super Jump raises the jump speed from -16 to -22. Only one power-up is active at a time, and its name is shown on the screen. In this version the power-ups are triggered by key presses; there are no power-up items placed in the levels.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 2998 0 R
/Next 3006 0 R
>>
endobj
3003 0 obj
<<
/Title <4D6172696F20416476656E7475726520852054686520546872656520576F726C6473>
/Dest [38 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3007 0 R
/Next 2999 0 R
>>
endobj
3004 0 obj
<<
/Title (Keyboard input. Two methods are used. The movement and jump keys \(Left, Right, Space and Up\) are checked continuously with GetAsyncKeyState inside UpdateGame, so the character keeps moving while a key is held down. One-time commands such as Enter, I, R, Esc, S, H and J are handled)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2990 0 R
/Prev 3000 0 R
/Next 3005 0 R
>>
endobj
3005 0 obj
<<
/Title (Player movement, gravity and jumping. The player's position is stored in playerX and playerY, and the vertical speed is stored in velocityY. The normal walking speed is 5 pixels per update. Pressing a jump key while the player is standing on a surface \(onGround is true\) gives an upward speed of -16. In every update gravity adds 1 to velocityY, and the falling speed is limited to 15. The world is 2300 pixels wide, and the player cannot walk outside it. The value cameraX follows the player so that the 900-pixel-wide window always shows the part of the world around the character. Platforms and collision detection. Platforms are stored in a vector of the structure Platform. The ground is a long platform at the bottom of the world, and each world adds five floating platforms. The function Collision checks whether two rectangles overlap by comparing their edges; this is the basic form of rectangle \(bounding box\) collision detection. When the falling player overlaps a platform, the player is placed on top of it and onGround becomes true. If the player is moving upwards and hits the underside of a platform, the player is pushed below it and the upward movement stops.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2990 0 R
/Prev 3004 0 R
/Next 3001 0 R
>>
endobj
3006 0 obj
<<
/Title (Boss health and the final battle. In World 3 a purple boss is placed near the end of the level. It moves slowly between x = 1800 and x = 1980 and has a health value bossHealth of 3, which is shown above it as BOSS HP. Jumping on the boss from above reduces its health by one, makes the player bounce, adds 50 points and starts a short cooldown of 35 updates so that one landing is not counted several times. When the health reaches zero the boss disappears and 200 bonus points are added. Touching the boss in any other way calls LoseLife. While the boss is alive, the message DEFEAT THE BOSS FIRST is displayed and World 3 cannot be completed.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3002 0 R
/Next 3008 0 R
>>
endobj
3007 0 obj
<<
/Title (Figure 3: Forest World gameplay at the start of the level: the player, the trees in the background, coins, platforms and a moving enemy, with Score, Lives and World shown at the top-left corner.)
/Dest [37 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3009 0 R
/Next 3003 0 R
>>
endobj
3008 0 obj
<<
/Title (Level transitions, game over and victory. Each world ends with a finish flag at x = 2140, drawn as a white pole with a red flag. Reaching it in World 1 or World 2 calls CompleteStage; in World 3 it works only after the boss has been defeated. The game then shows a level-complete screen \(for example, WORLD 1 COMPLETED!\) for 100 updates, about 1.6 seconds, before NextStage loads the next world. After World 3 the victory screen appears with the final score. If the lives reach zero, the game-over screen appears instead. On both screens, R restarts the game and Esc returns to the main menu.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3006 0 R
/Next 3010 0 R
>>
endobj
3009 0 obj
<<
/Title (Figure 2: Instructions Screen \(HOW TO PLAY\), which is opened by pressing I on the main menu.)
/Dest [37 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3011 0 R
/Next 3007 0 R
>>
endobj
3010 0 obj
<<
/Title (Graphics and appearance of the worlds. Everything on the screen is drawn with GDI. DrawRectangle fills a rectangle with a solid brush, DrawCircle draws an ellipse, DrawTextCenter creates an Arial font and writes centred text, and TextOutA writes the score line. The player is made from rectangles and a small circle, enemies are red circles with white eyes, coins are yellow circles and the finish flag is made from two rectangles. The Forest World has a light-blue sky and green trees, the Desert World has an orange sky and a yellow sun, and the Ice Kingdom has a pale-blue sky with pale circles that look like snow mounds. The platforms use the same brown body and green top in every world. Emoji characters are not used, because they did not display properly in the Windows graphics environment; shapes drawn with GDI are used instead.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3008 0 R
/Next 3012 0 R
>>
endobj
